window.docContents[236].resolve({"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Canonical-distributions--Fundamental-solutions":{"contents":"Let  be open, let  be continuous and linear, and let . The distribution  is a fundamental solution for\n at  precisely whenThe membership condition is mathematically essential. Mathlib defines\n on  by evaluation of test functions at ; because\nevery such test function vanishes off , this functional is zero when\n. Recording  prevents the homogeneous equation\n from being mislabeled as a point-source equation at an exterior pole.\nThe predicate otherwise states only the distribution equation and is\nindependent of any representative of .\n\nThe fundamental-solution predicate is equivalent to the conjunctionThus a proved fundamental-solution predicate supplies both a valid interior\npole and the action formula. Conversely, interiority together with the family\nof test-function identities determines the distribution equality.\n\nThe forward implication projects the interiority condition and applies the\ndistribution equality to an arbitrary test function. The Dirac action formula\nreduces the right side to . Conversely, retain the supplied proof\nthat  and use extensionality: continuous linear functionals are\nequal when they agree on every test function.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Distributions and Fourier Analysis\u0009Canonical distributions","header":"3.4.1.6. Fundamental solutions","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Canonical-distributions--Fundamental-solutions"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Weak-Waves-and-Evolution--Higher-Regularity-and-Compatibility--Identifying-the-Regular-Wave":{"contents":"The differentiated weak limits are derivatives of one solution, and elliptic\nlifting applies to its spatial slices. Here the time-regularity and\nspatial-regularity arguments meet.\n\nFor an autonomous operator, suppose the original limit is a weak wave, the\nforcing has a weak time derivative, and the original and differentiated\nGalerkin families have common weak limits with their exact derivative links.\nThe theorem identifies , , and a third weak time derivative.\nUniform essential bounds on the differentiated families pass to the limit.\n\nTest the Galerkin identity relating each trajectory to its time derivative\nagainst smooth compactly supported time functions. Weak convergence passes\nthat distributional identity to the paired limits, identifying the latter as\nsuccessive weak derivatives of one function. Then differentiate\n: autonomy gives\n in . The almost-everywhere norm-bound\nsets are closed and convex in , so their weak closedness preserves the\nuniform essential bounds.\n\nFor the pivot space , the paired-limit theorem identifies\nthe dual-valued acceleration with the pivot image of an -valued\nclass and preserves its essential spatial  bound. That is the input the\nelliptic lift needs at almost every time.\n\nLet  and let the domain carry the operational C2BoundaryAtlas.\nAssume uniformly elliptic  principal coefficients with global\n representatives. For a time- trajectory in \nsatisfying the weak wave equation with spatial  forcing and\nacceleration, essential bounds on those two spatial norms give an essentially\nbounded  lift. The elliptic solver's operator norm gives the quantitative\nestimate.\n\nFor almost every time, rewrite the wave equation as the elliptic problem\n. The global elliptic regularity solver supplies an\n representative of that spatial slice, and uniqueness identifies it\nwith . Measurability of the bounded solver assembles these slices into\na Bochner representative, while the operator norm and the two essential\n bounds give the essential-supremum estimate.\n\nAssembling the pieces gives the existence theorem. Symmetry of the spatial\noperator is assumed because the chain theorem invoked is the symmetric one.\n\nUnder the explicit compatibility, stable-density, coercivity, Ritz, and\nelliptic-lifting hypotheses, and assuming the spatial operator is symmetric,\nthere exists a weak wave with the prescribed initial data and an essentially\nbounded Sobolev time jet of order  whose head agrees almost everywhere with\nthe displacement and whose first time derivative is the velocity. For a nonsymmetric operator,\nthe corresponding steps are the\nnonsymmetric Galerkin chain\nand the higher-order lift.\n\nThe compatibility data initialize all differentiated Galerkin systems. The\nfinite common weak-wave chain supplies the time derivatives, and the assumed\nelliptic lift raises spatial order two derivatives at a time. Induction\nassembles the resulting representatives into one essentially bounded\nSobolev time jet.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Weak Waves and Evolution\u0009Higher Regularity and Compatibility","header":"7.3.3.3. Identifying the Regular Wave","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Weak-Waves-and-Evolution--Higher-Regularity-and-Compatibility--Identifying-the-Regular-Wave"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Classical-Heat-Flow":{"contents":"The heat operator, Gaussian kernel, initial convolution, Duhamel formula, and\ncomparison principles form one continuous construction. On bounded domains,\nenergy identities give backward uniqueness, and a localization argument shows\nthat every classical solution is smooth in the interior.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations","header":"6.2. Classical Heat Flow","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Classical-Heat-Flow"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--Second-Variations-and-Dirichlet-Energy--Quadratic-Energies":{"contents":"For a symmetric bounded bilinear form  and continuous load , the\nenergysatisfies the exact expansion\n\nExpand both bilinear arguments and the linear load, use symmetry on the two\nmixed terms, and collect coefficients.\n\nThe expansion is an exact Taylor formula: the term linear in  is\n, the quadratic term is , and there is no\nhigher-order remainder. The Fréchet derivative and the second variation can\ntherefore be read off directly.\n\nThe Fréchet derivative of the quadratic energy at  isor equivalently  as a continuous linear functional.\n\nMathlib's bilinear derivative rule differentiates B(u,u). Symmetry combines\nthe two resulting terms, while the load differentiates to itself.\n\nAt a local minimizer the derivative vanishes by Fermat's theorem, and the\nderivative formula turns that vanishing into an identity in every direction.\nBecause the energy is quadratic in , no separate differentiability\nhypothesis is needed here, in contrast to the semilinear energies, where\ndifferentiability of the potential has to be supplied before a minimizer can\nbe called a weak solution.\n\nEvery unconstrained local minimizer satisfies the weak Euler equation\n\nFermat's theorem makes the derivative vanish at a local minimum. Substituting\nthe derivative formula and evaluating the zero functional gives the weak\nequation.\n\nThe Euler equation used only the first derivative. The second derivative is\nwhere the quadratic structure pays off: because  depends affinely on\n, the Hessian does not depend on the base point at all, and the\nsecond-variation test can be evaluated without knowing the minimizer.\n\nThe second Fréchet derivative of the quadratic energy is independent of the\nbase point:\n\nThe first-variation map is the affine map ; its derivative\nis therefore the continuous linear map B.\n\nCombining the abstract second-order necessary condition with the base-point\nindependence of the Hessian gives a condition on  alone: a quadratic\nenergy can have a local minimizer only if  is positive semidefinite.\nCoercive forms satisfy this automatically.\n\nAt a local minimum, the second variation obeys the necessary condition\n\nIdentify the Hessian of the quadratic energy with B, then apply the abstract\ndiagonal nonnegativity theorem at the local minimum.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Minimization and Variations\u0009Second Variations and Dirichlet Energy","header":"9.3.2.3. Quadratic Energies","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--Second-Variations-and-Dirichlet-Energy--Quadratic-Energies"}});